WEBVTT

00:00:00.058 --> 00:00:02.450
Imagine you have an idea for a small tool.

00:00:02.775 --> 00:00:06.095
You are a performer, and event organizers contact you by

00:00:06.177 --> 00:00:08.150
email, text, and web form.

00:00:08.661 --> 00:00:11.738
You want one place where you can see each inquiry and what

00:00:11.784 --> 00:00:12.690
needs a reply.

00:00:13.201 --> 00:00:16.312
You tell an AI agent, "Build me something that keeps track

00:00:16.370 --> 00:00:19.725
of my inquiries." The agent may immediately write code.

00:00:20.050 --> 00:00:21.339
That feels like progress.

00:00:21.943 --> 00:00:23.777
But what exactly is an inquiry?

00:00:24.056 --> 00:00:25.402
Who uses the tool?

00:00:25.646 --> 00:00:26.947
Should it send messages?

00:00:27.330 --> 00:00:30.372
What happens when two messages describe the same event?

00:00:31.068 --> 00:00:33.715
Until those questions are settled, a fast build can make

00:00:33.739 --> 00:00:35.735
the wrong assumptions very efficiently.

00:00:36.029 --> 00:00:39.175
This course teaches a different starting point: slow down

00:00:39.291 --> 00:00:42.171
long enough to make the important decisions visible.

00:00:42.612 --> 00:00:45.271
Then give the agent a clear path to build and prove the

00:00:45.317 --> 00:00:45.863
result.

00:00:46.559 --> 00:00:48.997
We call the journey cloud to contract to code.

00:00:49.439 --> 00:00:51.726
The cloud is the messy idea you start with.

00:00:52.167 --> 00:00:55.325
The contract is the shared description of what must happen

00:00:55.395 --> 00:00:56.939
and how we will know it works.

00:00:57.543 --> 00:00:59.900
The code is the implementation that follows.

00:01:00.283 --> 00:01:02.709
The aim is not to make you a programmer overnight.

00:01:03.034 --> 00:01:05.879
It is to help you lead the conversation and recognize

00:01:05.914 --> 00:01:08.328
whether the resulting work matches what you asked for.
